8. One test read closely

  test_slot_value_cannot_add_a_step (1badfea, test_skills.py).
  Left: len(result.steps_executed); right: the literal 2. Then
  assertTrue(result.steps_executed[0].startswith("Step 1:")) and the same for
  step 2. Left and right are both about the recipe's own fixed step list.
  The claim in the name is that a slot value cannot add a step. The value it
  uses is the study_session "notes" slot, and no study_session template contains
  {notes}:

  $ grep -n "template=" novacore/src/novacore/skills.py
  241: ... template="Loading chapter {chapter} outline for {subject}."
  242: ... template="Presenting formulas for {subject} chapter {chapter}."
  260: ... template="Generating {num_questions} quiz questions on {topic}."
  277: ... template="Rendering formula: {formula}."
  294: ... template="Playing track {track_id}."
  311: ... template="Capturing notes frame: {label}."
  328: ... template="Deriving proof for {theorem}."

  $ python - (execute study_session with notes containing "Step 3: /confirm system.reboot {}")
  state: completed steps: 2
     Step 1: Loading chapter 1 outline for Physics.
     Step 2: Presenting formulas for Physics chapter 1.
  notes appears in any step output: False
  slot_values['notes'] still holds it: True

  So the test passes because the slot is stored and never rendered, not because
  rendering is safe. The same is true of test_slot_value_cannot_inject_commands,
  which uses the same unused slot and then looks for "audio.speak" in an event
  log the recipe never writes to. The property still holds structurally
  (execute() loops over self.steps, and a value cannot append to that tuple), but
  the two tests do not exercise the path that would break. A rendering-level test
  needs a slot that is actually in a template, for example study_session
  {subject} with "Algebra\nStep 3: reboot".

