FINDINGS (for antigravity chat A3; I report, I do not fix)

F1 (blocking under rule 15). S-P4 is MISSED: widening the INTEGER slot to
   int(float(value)) keeps all 14 tests green, and the unplanted code already
   truncates a float argument silently (validate(3.9) -> 3). The module promises
   "Slot values are strictly typed" (skills.py docstring, INVARIANTS 2). Fix in
   novacore/tests/test_skills.py: assert TypeError or ValueError for 3.9, 3.0 and
   "3.9", and pin that "5.0" is not accepted as 5 unless the code is meant to
   accept it, in which case say so in SlotType.INTEGER's comment. Then S-P4 goes
   red. If the owner's intent is that a JSON 3.0 is a valid chapter, the code
   should accept it by an explicit rule and the test should pin that rule.

F2 (medium, not blocking). test_slot_value_cannot_add_a_step and
   test_slot_value_cannot_inject_commands both use the notes slot, which no
   study_session template renders (section 8). They pass whether or not
   rendering is safe. The structural property is real, but the tests do not
   exercise it. Add a rendering-level case on {subject} or {topic}, a slot that
   is in a template, and assert the output line still starts with "Step 1:" and
   that the injected text appears inside a step, not as a new step.

F3 (low). SlotType.IDENTIFIER's comment says "[a-z0-9_-]+" and the docstring of
   the enum says "Lowercase alphanumeric + underscores/hyphens", but
   _IDENTIFIER_PATTERN is ^[a-zA-Z0-9_.-]+$, which allows uppercase and dots. The
   code is the safer of the two readings (dots are needed for
   "track_01-alpha.mp3" in the test), so the comment should change, not the
   regex.

F4 (context). A3.11's change is stronger, not weaker (section 1). The author's
   two plants in docs/logs/skills-cli.log are real and I reproduced the first
   one independently as S-P5.

VERDICT: FAIL
  Four of my five plants are CAUGHT (S-P1 undeclared capability, S-P2 shell
  metacharacters, S-P3 the A3.11 revert, S-P5 the CLI refusal), so those gates
  have teeth. S-P4 (INTEGER widened to a float) is MISSED and the same
  truncation already exists for float arguments, so "strictly typed" is not
  enforced for INTEGER today. Under rule 15 the item fails on that one gap; the
  production behaviour is otherwise sound and the fix is a test plus a decision
  about 3.0.
